<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thracians</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Thracians were an ancient Indo-European people of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, living in the lands of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, European <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. Divided into many tribes, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odrysians">Odrysians</a>, Getae and Bessi, they traded with the Greek colonies of the coast and were famed for their horsemen, gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> work and tombs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> in 46 CE, and their language died out in late antiquity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thracians.jpg" alt="Thracians" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ann Wuyts · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:National_Archaeological_Museum_Sofia_-_Bronze_Head_from_the_Golyama_Kosmatka_Tumulus_near_Shipka.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The exact origin of the Thracians is uncertain. It is believed that, like other Indo-European-speaking groups in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, they descended from a mixture of Proto-Indo-Europeans and Early European Farmers. During the 5th and 4th millennia BCE the inhabitants of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> formed different groups of indigenous people that the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> later named under the single ethnonym Thracians. Their culture emerged during the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>, which began about 3500 BCE, and the Getae and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacians">Dacians</a> developed from it.</p><p>The first record of the name is in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a>, where Thracians appear as allies of the Trojans. Only four Thracian inscriptions have been found, among them a gold ring from Ezerovo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, whose text of eight lines in Greek script has not been deciphered.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Greek colonies were founded along the Thracian coasts from the 8th century BCE, and Thracians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> lived side by side. According to Greek and Roman writers the Thracians remained largely disunited until the Odrysian kingdom was established in the early 5th century BCE by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teres-i">Teres I</a>, after the failure of the Persian invasion of Greece. Teres and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sitalces">Sitalces</a> expanded the kingdom, which allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a> on its side.</p><p>The kingdom showed signs of fatigue by 400 BCE, and Cotys I brought a brief revival that lasted until his murder in 360 BCE. Around 340 BCE it lost independence to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>, and around 330 BCE Seuthes III revived a much smaller state with a new capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuthopolis">Seuthopolis</a>. From the mid-2nd century BCE the Thracians faced gradual conquest by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. The Odrysian heartland became the client Sapaean kingdom, which was finally converted into the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thracia">Thracia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> described the Thracians as warlike and barbarian, yet favoured them as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>. They were skilled horsemen and lightly armed skirmishers with javelins, and Thracian peltasts had a notable influence in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. In 188 BCE near Cypsela they defeated the troops of the Roman consul Manlius Vulzon. Tattooing was a custom among both men and women.</p><p>Their religion was polytheistic. One cult was that of the Thracian horseman, a god of the underworld shown on funeral statues slaying a beast with a spear. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zalmoxis">Zalmoxis</a>, worshipped by the Getae and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacians">Dacians</a>, is the subject of debate over whether his cult was monotheistic.</p><p>Later invasions by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a>, Sarmatians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>, with hellenization, romanization and later slavicisation, remade the ethnicity, language and culture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>. Writers spoke of a separate Thracian nationality until the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, and the last reported use of a Thracian language was by monks in the 6th century CE. Late in the 4th century Nicetas of Remesiana brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> to the Bessi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thracians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It expanded into Emathia, Pieria, Bottiaea, Almopia, Mygdonia, Crestonia and Bisaltia, inhabited by <b>Thracians</b> and Phrygians among others, and into Eordaea and Upper Macedonia, home of the Greek tribes of the Lyncestae, Orestae and Elimiotae.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-macedon">Kingdom of Macedon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Herodotus judged the <b>Thracians</b> the most numerous people in the world after the Indians and thought they would be invincible if they were ever united under one ruler, but for most of their history they lived in separate tribes under their own chiefs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/odrysian-kingdom">Odrysian Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Vyacheslav Ivanov and Vladimir Toporov saw the religion as an outgrowth of Proto-Indo-European religion, close to the beliefs of the Balts, <b>Thracians</b> and Phrygians.</blockquote><a href="/religion/slavic-paganism">Slavic paganism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In the third century BCE the largest group is neither: 169 chiefdoms and confederations, most of them the Gauls, <b>Thracians</b>, Illyrians and other peoples of Iron Age Europe.</blockquote><a href="/essays/who-should-rule">Who Should Rule?</a></li><li><blockquote>Most were foreigners, <b>Thracians</b>, Carians, Scythians, Syrians and others, bought from traders or taken in war, and their children were born slaves.</blockquote><a href="/societies/athens">Social Hierarchy of Classical Athens</a></li><li><blockquote>A first settlement at Abdera by Clazomenae c. 654 BCE was driven out by <b>Thracians</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/abdera">Abdera</a></li></ul></section>
